    shortDescription: "Explain property checking.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `obj.hasOwnProperty('prop')` to check own property. Use `'prop' in obj` to check prototype chain. For safety, use `Object.hasOwn(obj, 'prop')` (ES2022).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you check if an object has a property, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `obj.hasOwnProperty('prop')` to check own property.\n2. Use `'prop' in obj` to check prototype chain.\n3. For safety, use `Object.hasOwn(obj, 'prop')` (ES2022).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Use `obj.hasOwnProperty('prop')` to check own property.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Use `obj.hasOwnProperty('prop')` to check own property.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
