    slug: "modules-js",
    shortDescription: "Explain modules.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nModules are a way to split code into separate files, each with its own scope. They use `export` and `import` statements (ES modules). Modules are loaded asynchronously, and they support tree-shaking.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Modules are a way to split code into separate files, each with its own scope.\n2. They use `export` and `import` statements (ES modules).\n3. Modules are loaded asynchronously, and they support tree-shaking.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Modules are a way to split code into separate files, each with its own scope.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Modules are a way to split code into separate files, each with its own scope.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
