    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStatic imports are hoisted and loaded before execution. Dynamic imports (`import()`) are asynchronous and load at runtime, enabling code splitting and lazy loading.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between dynamic import and static import and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Static imports are hoisted and loaded before execution.\n2. Dynamic imports (`import()`) are asynchronous and load at runtime, enabling code splitting and lazy loading.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Static imports are hoisted and loaded before execution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Static imports are hoisted and loaded before execution.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
