    question: "What is the difference between Node.js modules and ES modules?",
    slug: "node-vs-es-modules",
    shortDescription: "Compare module systems.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNode.js uses CommonJS (`require`/`module.exports`) by default. ES modules (`import`/`export`) are the standard. Node.js now supports ES modules with `.mjs` extension or `\"type\": \"module\"` in package.json.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between node.js modules and es modules and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Node.js uses CommonJS (`require`/`module.exports`) by default.\n2. ES modules (`import`/`export`) are the standard.\n3. Node.js now supports ES modules with `.mjs` extension or `\"type\": \"module\"` in package.json.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Node.js uses CommonJS (`require`/`module.exports`) by default.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Node.js uses CommonJS (`require`/`module.exports`) by default.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
