    slug: "throw-vs-return",
    shortDescription: "Compare throw and return.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`throw` raises an exception that can be caught; `return` exits the function with a value. `throw` can be caught by `catch`; `return` just returns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `throw` and `return` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `throw` raises an exception that can be caught; `return` exits the function with a value.\n2. `throw` can be caught by `catch`; `return` just returns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. `throw` raises an exception that can be caught; `return` exits the function with a value.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `throw` raises an exception that can be caught; `return` exits the function with a value.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
