    slug: "debouncing-throttling",
    shortDescription: "Explain debouncing and throttling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDebouncing delays function execution until after a quiet period; throttling limits execution rate (e.g., once per second). Used for scroll/resize events.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain debouncing and throttling accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Debouncing delays function execution until after a quiet period; throttling limits execution rate (e.g., once per second).\n2. Used for scroll/resize events.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Debouncing delays function execution until after a quiet period; throttling limits execution rate (e.g., once per second).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Debouncing delays function execution until after a quiet period; throttling limits execution rate (e.g., once per second).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
