    question: "What is the difference between debouncing and throttling?",
    slug: "debounce-vs-throttle",
    shortDescription: "Compare debounce and throttle.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDebouncing: execute after delay from last call; throttling: execute at most once per interval. Debouncing for input; throttling for scroll.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between debouncing and throttling and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Debouncing: execute after delay from last call; throttling: execute at most once per interval.\n2. Debouncing for input; throttling for scroll.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Debouncing: execute after delay from last call; throttling: execute at most once per interval.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Debouncing: execute after delay from last call; throttling: execute at most once per interval.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
