    slug: "memory-leak-js",
    shortDescription: "Explain memory leaks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMemory leaks occur when objects are not garbage collected due to lingering references. Causes: accidental globals, unremoved event listeners, closures holding large data, and detached DOM nodes. Prevent by cleaning up listeners and clearing references.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a memory leak and how to prevent it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Memory leaks occur when objects are not garbage collected due to lingering references.\n2. Causes: accidental globals, unremoved event listeners, closures holding large data, and detached DOM nodes.\n3. Prevent by cleaning up listeners and clearing references.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Memory leaks occur when objects are not garbage collected due to lingering references.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Memory leaks occur when objects are not garbage collected due to lingering references.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
