    shortDescription: "Compare time measurement.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`performance.now()` returns high-resolution, monotonic time (measured in milliseconds) from navigation start, suitable for performance measurements. `Date.now()` gives system time (affected by system clock adjustments).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `performance.now()` and `date.now()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `performance.now()` returns high-resolution, monotonic time (measured in milliseconds) from navigation start, suitable for performance measurements.\n2. `Date.now()` gives system time (affected by system clock adjustments).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. `performance.now()` returns high-resolution, monotonic time (measured in milliseconds) from navigation start, suitable for performance measurements.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `performance.now()` returns high-resolution, monotonic time (measured in milliseconds) from navigation start, suitable for performance measurements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
