    slug: "async-defer",
    shortDescription: "Explain script loading.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`async` loads script asynchronously and executes as soon as it's ready. `defer` loads script asynchronously but executes after HTML parsing. Both prevent render blocking.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `async/defer` attribute on `<script>` tags accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `async` loads script asynchronously and executes as soon as it's ready.\n2. `defer` loads script asynchronously but executes after HTML parsing.\n3. Both prevent render blocking.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nAn `async` function can await a fetch promise; the current function yields while the I/O is pending and continues when the promise settles.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `async` loads script asynchronously and executes as soon as it's ready.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
