    slug: "localstorage-vs-cookies",
    shortDescription: "Compare storage mechanisms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`localStorage` stores data locally with larger storage (~5MB), sent only via JS. Cookies are smaller (4KB), sent with every HTTP request, and can be set with expiration. Cookies can be used for server-side.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `localstorage` and `cookies` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `localStorage` stores data locally with larger storage (~5MB), sent only via JS.\n2. Cookies are smaller (4KB), sent with every HTTP request, and can be set with expiration.\n3. Cookies can be used for server-side.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. `localStorage` stores data locally with larger storage (~5MB), sent only via JS.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `localStorage` stores data locally with larger storage (~5MB), sent only via JS.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
