    slug: "oop-principles",
    shortDescription: "The interviewer expects a clear definition of the four pillars.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe four main principles are Encapsulation (bundling data and methods), Inheritance (creating new classes based on existing ones), Polymorphism (ability to take many forms, i.e., method overloading/overriding), and Abstraction (hiding implementation details).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The four main principles are Encapsulation (bundling data and methods), Inheritance (creating new classes based on existing ones), Polymorphism (ability to take many forms, i.e., method overloading/overriding), and Abstraction (hiding implementation details).\n2. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The four main principles are Encapsulation (bundling data and methods), Inheritance (creating new classes based on existing ones), Polymorphism (ability to take many forms, i.e., method overloading/overriding), and Abstraction (hiding implementation details).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Java Developer, what are the main principles of object-oriented programming (oop) can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
