    question: "What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface?",
    slug: "abstract-class-vs-interface",
    shortDescription: "Compare their features, especially post-Java 8.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn abstract class can have instance variables and constructors, and can define state. An interface, before Java 8, only had public abstract methods. From Java 8 onward, interfaces can have default and static methods. A class can implement multiple interfaces but can only extend one class.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between an abstract class and an interface and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An abstract class can have instance variables and constructors, and can define state.\n2. An interface, before Java 8, only had public abstract methods.\n3. From Java 8 onward, interfaces can have default and static methods.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can depend on an interface such as `PaymentGateway`, allowing a production adapter and a test double to implement the same contract.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An abstract class can have instance variables and constructors, and can define state.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
