    shortDescription: "Explain its usage for variables, methods, and blocks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`static` indicates that a member belongs to the class itself rather than to any specific instance. Static variables are shared across all instances. Static methods can be called without creating an object, but they cannot access instance variables directly.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `static` keyword in Java accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `static` indicates that a member belongs to the class itself rather than to any specific instance.\n2. Static variables are shared across all instances.\n3. Static methods can be called without creating an object, but they cannot access instance variables directly.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nA `static` counter belongs to the class rather than each object, so all instances observe the same class-level value.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `static` indicates that a member belongs to the class itself rather than to any specific instance.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
