    shortDescription: "Part of SOLID principles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSRP states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility. This improves maintainability and testability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP). accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.\n2. This improves maintainability and testability.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
