    shortDescription: "Explain the 'Has-A' vs 'Is-A' relationship.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInheritance represents an 'Is-A' relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal). Composition represents a 'Has-A' relationship (e.g., Car has an Engine). Composition offers more flexibility and is favored over inheritance to avoid tight coupling and the diamond problem.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between composition and inheritance and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Inheritance represents an 'Is-A' relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal).\n2. Composition represents a 'Has-A' relationship (e.g., Car has an Engine).\n3. Composition offers more flexibility and is favored over inheritance to avoid tight coupling and the diamond problem.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Inheritance represents an 'Is-A' relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Inheritance represents an 'Is-A' relationship (e.g., Dog is an Animal).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference."