    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe `volatile` keyword ensures that a variable's value is always read from main memory, not from thread-local caches. It guarantees visibility of changes across threads but does not guarantee atomicity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `volatile` keyword accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The `volatile` keyword ensures that a variable's value is always read from main memory, not from thread-local caches.\n2. It guarantees visibility of changes across threads but does not guarantee atomicity.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The `volatile` keyword ensures that a variable's value is always read from main memory, not from thread-local caches.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The `volatile` keyword ensures that a variable's value is always read from main memory, not from thread-local caches.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
