    slug: "arraylist-vs-linkedlist",
    shortDescription: "Compare time complexity and use cases.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`ArrayList` uses a dynamic array, providing O(1) random access but O(n) insertion/deletion in the middle. `LinkedList` uses a doubly-linked list, providing O(1) insertion/deletion at ends and O(n) random access. `LinkedList` is better for frequent insertion/deletion; `ArrayList` is better for frequent access.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `arraylist` and `linkedlist` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `ArrayList` uses a dynamic array, providing O(1) random access but O(n) insertion/deletion in the middle.\n2. `LinkedList` uses a doubly-linked list, providing O(1) insertion/deletion at ends and O(n) random access.\n3. `LinkedList` is better for frequent insertion/deletion; `ArrayList` is better for frequent access.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `ArrayList` uses a dynamic array, providing O(1) random access but O(n) insertion/deletion in the middle.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `ArrayList` uses a dynamic array, providing O(1) random access but O(n) insertion/deletion in the middle.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
