    shortDescription: "Explain hashing, buckets, and collision handling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`HashMap` stores key-value pairs. It uses an array of `Node` (buckets). The key's `hashCode()` determines the bucket index. In case of collisions (same bucket), entries are stored as a linked list (or tree if the list grows beyond a threshold) in the same bucket. When retrieving, the `equals()` method is used to find the exact key.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `HashMap` work internally accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `HashMap` stores key-value pairs.\n2. It uses an array of `Node` (buckets).\n3. The key's `hashCode()` determines the bucket index.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA `HashMap<Long, Employee>` is suitable for expected O(1) lookup when a map is not concurrently mutated; use synchronization or a concurrent collection when shared writes are required.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `HashMap` stores key-value pairs.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
