    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`HashMap` is not synchronized (not thread-safe) and allows one `null` key and multiple `null` values. `HashTable` is synchronized (thread-safe) and does not allow `null` keys or values. `HashMap` is generally preferred in non-concurrent scenarios.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `hashmap` and `hashtable` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `HashMap` is not synchronized (not thread-safe) and allows one `null` key and multiple `null` values.\n2. `HashTable` is synchronized (thread-safe) and does not allow `null` keys or values.\n3. `HashMap` is generally preferred in non-concurrent scenarios.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA `HashMap<Long, Employee>` is suitable for expected O(1) lookup when a map is not concurrently mutated; use synchronization or a concurrent collection when shared writes are required.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `HashMap` is not synchronized (not thread-safe) and allows one `null` key and multiple `null` values.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
