    question: "What is the difference between `map` and `flatMap` in Streams?",
    slug: "map-vs-flatmap",
    shortDescription: "Compare the mapping operations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`map` applies a function to each element and returns a Stream of the results (one-to-one mapping). `flatMap` applies a function that returns a Stream for each element, and then flattens these streams into a single Stream (one-to-many mapping).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `map` and `flatmap` in streams and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `map` applies a function to each element and returns a Stream of the results (one-to-one mapping).\n2. `flatMap` applies a function that returns a Stream for each element, and then flattens these streams into a single Stream (one-to-many mapping).\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `map` applies a function to each element and returns a Stream of the results (one-to-one mapping).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `map` applies a function to each element and returns a Stream of the results (one-to-one mapping).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
