    shortDescription: "Explain memory allocation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHeap memory is used for dynamic allocation of objects and JRE classes. It is shared across all threads. Stack memory is used for execution of a thread and stores method-specific values and references to objects (local primitives and object references). Stack is thread-private and has a fixed size.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between heap and stack memory and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Heap memory is used for dynamic allocation of objects and JRE classes.\n2. It is shared across all threads.\n3. Stack memory is used for execution of a thread and stores method-specific values and references to objects (local primitives and object references).\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Heap memory is used for dynamic allocation of objects and JRE classes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Heap memory is used for dynamic allocation of objects and JRE classes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
