    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMemory leaks occur when objects are unintentionally kept alive. You can diagnose them using tools like `jmap` (heap dumps), `jvisualvm`, `jconsole`, or profilers like YourKit/Async Profiler. Heap dumps can be analyzed to find object retention paths using MAT (Memory Analyzer Tool) or JProfiler.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you diagnose a memory leak in java, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Memory leaks occur when objects are unintentionally kept alive.\n2. You can diagnose them using tools like `jmap` (heap dumps), `jvisualvm`, `jconsole`, or profilers like YourKit/Async Profiler.\n3. Heap dumps can be analyzed to find object retention paths using MAT (Memory Analyzer Tool) or JProfiler.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a Java backend service can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Memory leaks occur when objects are unintentionally kept alive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Memory leaks occur when objects are unintentionally kept alive.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
