    question: "What are the different fetch strategies in JPA? (`EAGER` vs `LAZY`)",
    slug: "eager-vs-lazy",
    shortDescription: "Explain the loading strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`EAGER` loads related entities immediately (in the same query or join). `LAZY` loads related entities on-demand (proxies are used, and data is fetched when accessed). `LAZY` is preferred to avoid loading unnecessary data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the boundary between the two concepts, not just their definitions. The interviewer is looking for the practical consequence of choosing one over the other.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `EAGER` loads related entities immediately (in the same query or join).\n2. `LAZY` loads related entities on-demand (proxies are used, and data is fetched when accessed).\n3. `LAZY` is preferred to avoid loading unnecessary data.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `EAGER` loads related entities immediately (in the same query or join).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. `EAGER` loads related entities immediately (in the same query or join).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
