    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@BeforeEach` runs before each test method, used for common setup. `@BeforeAll` runs once for the entire test class, used for expensive setup (must be static).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@before` and `@beforeclass` (or `@beforeeach` and `@beforeall`) and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@BeforeEach` runs before each test method, used for common setup.\n2. `@BeforeAll` runs once for the entire test class, used for expensive setup (must be static).\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@BeforeEach` runs before each test method, used for common setup.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@BeforeEach` runs before each test method, used for common setup.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
