    slug: "escape-analysis",
    shortDescription: "Explain JIT optimization.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEscape analysis is a technique where the JIT compiler determines if an object is confined to a single method (does not escape). If it doesn't escape, the JVM can allocate it on the stack (rather than the heap) and even eliminate synchronization, improving performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the concept of `escape analysis` in JVM accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Escape analysis is a technique where the JIT compiler determines if an object is confined to a single method (does not escape).\n2. If it doesn't escape, the JVM can allocate it on the stack (rather than the heap) and even eliminate synchronization, improving performance.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Escape analysis is a technique where the JIT compiler determines if an object is confined to a single method (does not escape).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Escape analysis is a technique where the JIT compiler determines if an object is confined to a single method (does not escape).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
