    shortDescription: "Explain immutability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPrefer `val` for immutability. Use `var` only when state must change. This reduces bugs and improves readability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in when to use `val` vs `var` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Prefer `val` for immutability.\n2. Use `var` only when state must change.\n3. This reduces bugs and improves readability.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Prefer `val` for immutability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Kotlin, when to use `val` vs `var` can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
