    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It represents a single instance of a running process and can contain one or more containers that share networking (same IP) and storage volumes. Containers in a Pod are always co-located and co-scheduled.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a Pod in Kubernetes accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.\n2. It represents a single instance of a running process and can contain one or more containers that share networking (same IP) and storage volumes.\n3. Containers in a Pod are always co-located and co-scheduled.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
