    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Deployment is a Kubernetes resource that manages stateless applications. It provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets, enabling rolling updates, rollbacks, scaling, and self-healing. It is the recommended way to manage stateless Pods.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a Deployment and why is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Deployment is a Kubernetes resource that manages stateless applications.\n2. It provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets, enabling rolling updates, rollbacks, scaling, and self-healing.\n3. It is the recommended way to manage stateless Pods.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nUpdate the image tag in a Deployment and let the controller roll out replacement Pods while readiness checks prevent unready Pods from receiving traffic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Deployment is a Kubernetes resource that manages stateless applications.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
