    shortDescription: "Explain service types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that gives a stable virtual endpoint for a changing set of Pods, normally selected by labels. It provides service discovery through DNS and distributes traffic across eligible backend endpoints. Common Service types are ClusterIP for internal access, NodePort for exposing a port on each node, LoadBalancer for requesting an external load balancer when supported by the environment, and ExternalName for DNS-based aliasing to an external name.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a Service in Kubernetes and what are its types accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that gives a stable virtual endpoint for a changing set of Pods, normally selected by labels.\n2. It provides service discovery through DNS and distributes traffic across eligible backend endpoints.\n3. Common Service types are ClusterIP for internal access, NodePort for exposing a port on each node, LoadBalancer for requesting an external load balancer when supported by the environment, and ExternalName for DNS-based aliasing to an external name.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that gives a stable virtual endpoint for a changing set of Pods, normally selected by labels.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that gives a stable virtual endpoint for a changing set of Pods, normally selected by labels.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
