    shortDescription: "Explain their roles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Pod runs application containers. A Service provides a stable endpoint to access a group of Pods, enabling load balancing and service discovery. Pods are ephemeral; Services provide persistence.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a service and a pod and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Pod runs application containers.\n2. A Service provides a stable endpoint to access a group of Pods, enabling load balancing and service discovery.\n3. Pods are ephemeral; Services provide persistence.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A Pod runs application containers.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Pod runs application containers.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
