    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDeployments are for stateless applications with interchangeable Pods. StatefulSets are for stateful applications where Pods have unique identities and persistent storage. StatefulSets provide ordered startup/shutdown and stable network identities.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a deployment and a statefulset and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Deployments are for stateless applications with interchangeable Pods.\n2. StatefulSets are for stateful applications where Pods have unique identities and persistent storage.\n3. StatefulSets provide ordered startup/shutdown and stable network identities.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nUpdate the image tag in a Deployment and let the controller roll out replacement Pods while readiness checks prevent unready Pods from receiving traffic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Deployments are for stateless applications with interchangeable Pods.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
