    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKubernetes uses a flat network model where every Pod gets its own IP address, and all Pods can communicate without NAT. The network model is implemented via CNI plugins (e.g., Calico, Flannel, Cilium). Services provide stable endpoints and load balancing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Kubernetes networking accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Kubernetes uses a flat network model where every Pod gets its own IP address, and all Pods can communicate without NAT.\n2. The network model is implemented via CNI plugins (e.g., Calico, Flannel, Cilium).\n3. Services provide stable endpoints and load balancing.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Kubernetes uses a flat network model where every Pod gets its own IP address, and all Pods can communicate without NAT.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Kubernetes uses a flat network model where every Pod gets its own IP address, and all Pods can communicate without NAT.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
