    slug: "cni-definition",
    shortDescription: "Explain the plugin interface.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe Container Network Interface (CNI) is a specification and plugin model for configuring networking for containers. Kubernetes relies on a CNI implementation to provide Pod networking, such as assigning Pod IP addresses, configuring interfaces and routes, and optionally enforcing network policy. Examples include Cilium, Calico, and Flannel. CNI is the networking integration point; Kubernetes itself defines the Pod networking model but does not implement one universal network plugin.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the Container Network Interface (CNI) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The Container Network Interface (CNI) is a specification and plugin model for configuring networking for containers.\n2. Kubernetes relies on a CNI implementation to provide Pod networking, such as assigning Pod IP addresses, configuring interfaces and routes, and optionally enforcing network policy.\n3. Examples include Cilium, Calico, and Flannel.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The Container Network Interface (CNI) is a specification and plugin model for configuring networking for containers.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The Container Network Interface (CNI) is a specification and plugin model for configuring networking for containers.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
