    slug: "service-types",
    shortDescription: "Compare the service types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClusterIP: internal IP accessible only within the cluster. NodePort: exposes the service on a static port (30000-32767) on each node. LoadBalancer: provisions an external load balancer (cloud) to route traffic to the service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between clusterip, nodeport, and loadbalancer and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ClusterIP: internal IP accessible only within the cluster.\n2. NodePort: exposes the service on a static port (30000-32767) on each node.\n3. LoadBalancer: provisions an external load balancer (cloud) to route traffic to the service.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. ClusterIP: internal IP accessible only within the cluster.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. ClusterIP: internal IP accessible only within the cluster.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
