    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA NetworkPolicy is a namespaced Kubernetes resource that controls which network connections are allowed to and from selected Pods. Policies can define ingress and egress rules using Pod selectors, namespace selectors, IP blocks, and ports. NetworkPolicy is only effective when the installed network implementation supports it, and policy behavior is additive: allowed traffic is the result of the applicable policies, not a single global rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a NetworkPolicy and how is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A NetworkPolicy is a namespaced Kubernetes resource that controls which network connections are allowed to and from selected Pods.\n2. Policies can define ingress and egress rules using Pod selectors, namespace selectors, IP blocks, and ports.\n3. NetworkPolicy is only effective when the installed network implementation supports it, and policy behavior is additive: allowed traffic is the result of the applicable policies, not a single global rule.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A NetworkPolicy is a namespaced Kubernetes resource that controls which network connections are allowed to and from selected Pods.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A NetworkPolicy is a namespaced Kubernetes resource that controls which network connections are allowed to and from selected Pods.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
