    slug: "kubernetes-dns",
    shortDescription: "Explain service discovery.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKubernetes uses CoreDNS (or kube-dns) as the cluster DNS. It automatically creates DNS records for Services and Pods. For example, a Service `my-service` in namespace `default` is accessible as `my-service.default.svc.cluster.local`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain DNS work in Kubernetes accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Kubernetes uses CoreDNS (or kube-dns) as the cluster DNS.\n2. It automatically creates DNS records for Services and Pods.\n3. For example, a Service `my-service` in namespace `default` is accessible as `my-service.default.svc.cluster.local`.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Kubernetes uses CoreDNS (or kube-dns) as the cluster DNS.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Kubernetes uses CoreDNS (or kube-dns) as the cluster DNS.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
