    slug: "persistent-storage-types",
    shortDescription: "List the options.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKubernetes supports ephemeral volumes such as `emptyDir`, node-local options such as `hostPath`, and persistent storage exposed through PersistentVolumes and CSI drivers. CSI-backed storage can represent block or file storage from cloud providers, SAN/NAS systems, and other storage platforms. The appropriate choice depends on whether data must survive Pod recreation, whether it must move between nodes, the required access mode, and the storage system's durability and performance characteristics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because list the options. The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Kubernetes supports ephemeral volumes such as `emptyDir`, node-local options such as `hostPath`, and persistent storage exposed through PersistentVolumes and CSI drivers.\n2. CSI-backed storage can represent block or file storage from cloud providers, SAN/NAS systems, and other storage platforms.\n3. The appropriate choice depends on whether data must survive Pod recreation, whether it must move between nodes, the required access mode, and the storage system's durability and performance characteristics.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Kubernetes supports ephemeral volumes such as `emptyDir`, node-local options such as `hostPath`, and persistent storage exposed through PersistentVolumes and CSI drivers.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a container platform, what types of persistent storage are supported in kubernetes affects deployment, networking, storage, health, or runtime isolation. The practical decision depends on the workload and failure/recovery requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
