    question: "What is the difference between ReadWriteOnce and ReadWriteMany access modes?",
    slug: "access-modes",
    shortDescription: "Explain the modes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nReadWriteOnce (RWO) means a volume can be mounted read-write by a single node at a time; it does not necessarily mean only one Pod can use it if those Pods are on the same node. ReadWriteMany (RWX) allows a volume to be mounted read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously. ReadOnlyMany (ROX) is also an access mode. Whether a mode is actually supported depends on the storage backend and its CSI driver.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between readwriteonce and readwritemany access modes and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ReadWriteOnce (RWO) means a volume can be mounted read-write by a single node at a time; it does not necessarily mean only one Pod can use it if those Pods are on the same node.\n2. ReadWriteMany (RWX) allows a volume to be mounted read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously.\n3. ReadOnlyMany (ROX) is also an access mode.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. ReadWriteOnce (RWO) means a volume can be mounted read-write by a single node at a time; it does not necessarily mean only one Pod can use it if those Pods are on the same node.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. ReadWriteOnce (RWO) means a volume can be mounted read-write by a single node at a time; it does not necessarily mean only one Pod can use it if those Pods are on the same node.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
