    slug: "secret-vs-configmap",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA ConfigMap is intended for non-sensitive configuration, while a Secret is intended for sensitive information. Both can be injected into Pods as environment variables or mounted as files. Kubernetes Secret values are not inherently encrypted merely because they are Secrets; base64 encoding is not encryption. Access should be restricted with RBAC and, where required, encryption at rest and external secret-management solutions should be used.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a secret and a configmap and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A ConfigMap is intended for non-sensitive configuration, while a Secret is intended for sensitive information.\n2. Both can be injected into Pods as environment variables or mounted as files.\n3. Kubernetes Secret values are not inherently encrypted merely because they are Secrets; base64 encoding is not encryption.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A ConfigMap is intended for non-sensitive configuration, while a Secret is intended for sensitive information.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A ConfigMap is intended for non-sensitive configuration, while a Secret is intended for sensitive information.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
