    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse Secrets with encryption at rest. Use external secrets management tools (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) to store secrets outside the cluster. Use CSI drivers to mount secrets as volumes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you manage secrets securely in kubernetes, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use Secrets with encryption at rest.\n2. Use external secrets management tools (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) to store secrets outside the cluster.\n3. Use CSI drivers to mount secrets as volumes.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use Secrets with encryption at rest.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use Secrets with encryption at rest.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
