    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTaints are applied to nodes to repel Pods. Tolerations are applied to Pods to allow them to be scheduled on nodes with matching taints. They are used to dedicate nodes for specific workloads (e.g., GPU nodes) or to prevent scheduling on certain nodes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Taints are applied to nodes to repel Pods.\n2. Tolerations are applied to Pods to allow them to be scheduled on nodes with matching taints.\n3. They are used to dedicate nodes for specific workloads (e.g., GPU nodes) or to prevent scheduling on certain nodes.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Taints are applied to nodes to repel Pods.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Taints are applied to nodes to repel Pods.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
