    slug: "affinity-types",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNode affinity schedules Pods based on node labels (e.g., `node-type=high-memory`). Pod affinity schedules Pods relative to other Pods (e.g., `same backend`). Pod anti-affinity prevents Pods from being scheduled together.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between node affinity and pod affinity and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Node affinity schedules Pods based on node labels (e.g., `node-type=high-memory`).\n2. Pod affinity schedules Pods relative to other Pods (e.g., `same backend`).\n3. Pod anti-affinity prevents Pods from being scheduled together.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Node affinity schedules Pods based on node labels (e.g., `node-type=high-memory`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Node affinity schedules Pods based on node labels (e.g., `node-type=high-memory`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
